# Full studies: baptism, Mary, saints, images, prayer, purgatory. Loaded by tools/vapol.py (exec).

D('1-peter',3,21,
 ctx="1 Peter 3:18–22: ‘Because Christ also died once for our sins, the just for the unjust: that he might offer us to God, being put to death indeed in the flesh, but enlivened in the spirit, in which also coming he preached to those spirits that were in prison: which had been some time incredulous, when they waited for the patience of God in the days of Noe, when the ark was a building: wherein a few, that is, eight souls, were saved by water. Whereunto baptism being of the like form, now saveth you also: not the putting away of the filth of the flesh, but the examination of a good conscience towards God by the resurrection of Jesus Christ.’",
 who="Baptists and many evangelicals hold that baptism is an outward sign of a salvation already received by faith, an act of obedience and public testimony, not a means of grace. They read ‘not the putting away of the filth of the flesh’ as saying that the water itself does nothing; what saves is the inner response of a good conscience, of which baptism is the figure.",
 lang="“Baptism… now saveth you” (hymas antitypon nyn sōzei baptisma): ‘baptism, the antitype, now saves you’. Baptism is the reality (antitypon) that the Flood prefigured (typos).\n\n“Examination” (eperōtēma): an appeal, pledge or request to God. Scholars debate whether it means the candidate’s pledge (answering the baptismal questions) or an appeal to God for a clean conscience. Either way it is part of baptism.\n\n“By the resurrection of Jesus Christ”: baptism saves through the power of the risen Christ.",
 steps=[
  ["Peter says baptism saves","The sentence’s main clause is ‘baptism now saves you’. Peter could not have said it more directly. What he excludes is a misunderstanding: baptism is not a bath that removes dirt from the body. It saves as a spiritual washing working through Christ’s resurrection."],
  ["The Flood typology implies efficacy","Noah’s family were ‘saved by water’ (3:20), carried through the waters that destroyed sin. Baptism is the antitype: the fulfilment, greater than the figure. If the type actually saved eight people, the antitype does not save less."],
  ["The rest of the New Testament agrees","‘Do penance, and be baptized every one of you… for the remission of your sins’ (Acts 2:38). ‘Be baptized, and wash away thy sins’ (Acts 22:16). ‘He saved us, by the laver of regeneration, and renovation of the Holy Ghost’ (Titus 3:5). ‘Unless a man be born again of water and the Holy Ghost’ (John 3:5). ‘Buried with him in baptism… quickened together with him’ (Colossians 2:12–13). ‘As many of you as have been baptized in Christ, have put on Christ’ (Galatians 3:27)."],
  ["Baptism is God’s work, received in faith","Catholics agree that baptism of an adult requires faith and repentance. The saving power comes from Christ’s resurrection, not from the water or the minister. But God has attached his grace to this sign, as he chose to heal Naaman through the Jordan (2 Kings 5:14)."],
  ["The early Church was unanimous","Every early Christian writer treats baptism as the moment of regeneration and forgiveness. A purely symbolic view does not appear until the Reformation era (Zwingli, Anabaptists), and Luther, Calvin and the Anglican Prayer Book all retained baptismal regeneration in some form."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Justin Martyr, First Apology 61 (c. 155)","Converts ‘are brought by us where there is water, and are regenerated in the same manner in which we were ourselves regenerated’."],
  ["Tertullian, On Baptism 1 (c. 200)","‘Happy is our sacrament of water, in that, by washing away the sins of our early blindness, we are set free into eternal life’ (paraphrased)."],
  ["Cyril of Jerusalem, Catechetical Lectures 3.10–12 (c. 350)","If any man receive not baptism, he has not salvation, except only martyrs, who even without the water receive the kingdom (paraphrased)."],
  ["Nicene-Constantinopolitan Creed (381)","‘We confess one baptism for the forgiveness of sins.’"]],
 obj=[
  ["“‘Not the removal of dirt’ shows the water does nothing.”","It shows baptism is not a physical washing. Peter immediately says what it is: an appeal to God for a good conscience, ‘by the resurrection of Jesus Christ’. The saving power is Christ’s, through the sacrament. Peter does not say ‘baptism does not save’; he says the opposite."],
  ["“Baptism is a figure, like the ark.”","The ark is the figure; baptism is the antitype, the reality. The Douay ‘of the like form’ translates antitypon, which in 1 Peter means the fulfilment."]],
 talk="“Peter literally says ‘baptism now saves you’. He explains he doesn’t mean a bath that cleans your skin; it saves through Jesus’ resurrection, as an appeal to God for a clean conscience. That’s Catholic teaching exactly: God does the saving, through the sacrament Jesus gave. Acts 2:38, Acts 22:16 and Titus 3:5 all say the same thing.”",
 avoid="Do not imply that baptism works without faith in adults; it requires faith and repentance (CCC 1253–1255).",
 read=["CCC 1213–1284","Council of Trent, Session 7, canons on baptism","Tertullian, On Baptism","Everett Ferguson (2009) Baptism in the Early Church. Grand Rapids: Eerdmans (a Church of Christ scholar)"])

D('mark',16,16,
 ctx="Mark 16:15–16, the risen Christ’s commission: ‘Go ye into the whole world, and preach the gospel to every creature. He that believeth and is baptized, shall be saved: but he that believeth not shall be condemned.’ Then follow the signs that will accompany believers (16:17–18). This passage belongs to the ‘longer ending’ of Mark (16:9–20), absent from two important 4th-century manuscripts (Sinaiticus and Vaticanus) but present in the great majority of manuscripts and quoted by Irenaeus (c. 180). The Catholic Church accepts it as canonical Scripture (Trent, Session 4).",
 who="Two uses. (1) Against baptism’s necessity: the second clause says ‘he that believeth not shall be condemned’, not ‘he that is not baptized’. So unbelief condemns; lack of baptism does not. (2) Against infant baptism: belief comes before baptism, and infants cannot believe.",
 lang="“He that believeth and is baptized” (ho pisteusas kai baptistheis): two aorist participles joined under one article: one person, believing and baptized.\n\nThe longer ending is canonical regardless of its authorship, according to Catholic teaching (and many Protestants accept it too).",
 steps=[
  ["The positive promise joins faith and baptism","‘He that believeth and is baptized shall be saved.’ The person who is saved is the one who both believes and is baptized. If baptism were irrelevant to salvation, Jesus would not have joined it to faith in the promise."],
  ["The second clause need not repeat baptism","An unbeliever would not seek baptism, so ‘he that believeth not’ covers the case completely. Compare: ‘whoever studies and sits the exam will pass; whoever does not study will fail’. Not mentioning the exam in the second clause does not make it optional."],
  ["Catholics also say unbelief, not lack of water, is what condemns","The Church teaches that God is not bound by the sacraments: those who die with the desire of baptism are saved (CCC 1258–1260). What condemns is the deliberate refusal of faith and of Christ’s means. The verse fits this exactly."],
  ["Infant baptism is not addressed here","The commission concerns adults hearing the gospel preached. Adults must believe before baptism. Infants of believing parents are baptized into the faith of the Church, as Israelite boys were circumcised on the eighth day into the covenant (Genesis 17:12; Colossians 2:11–12). Peter: ‘the promise is to you, and to your children’ (Acts 2:39). Households were baptized (Acts 16:15, 33; 18:8; 1 Corinthians 1:16)."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Irenaeus, Against Heresies II.22.4 (c. 180)","Christ came to save all who through him are born again to God: infants, children, boys, youths and old men (paraphrased)."],
  ["Origen, Commentary on Romans 5.9 (c. 246)","The Church received from the apostles the tradition of giving baptism even to infants (paraphrased)."],
  ["Cyprian, Letter 64 (58), to Fidus (c. 253)","A council of 66 bishops rules that infants need not wait until the eighth day to be baptized (paraphrased): infant baptism already taken for granted."]],
 obj=[
  ["“The longer ending of Mark isn’t in the oldest manuscripts.”","It is absent from two important manuscripts, but quoted by Irenaeus a century and a half earlier and found in the vast majority of manuscripts. Catholics accept it as canonical. And the same teaching appears in Acts 2:38, John 3:5 and 1 Peter 3:21."]],
 talk="“The saved person in that verse is the one who believes and is baptized; Jesus links them. The second half doesn’t need to say ‘and isn’t baptized’, because someone who refuses to believe won’t be baptized anyway. Catholics agree that what condemns is rejecting faith; but Jesus gave baptism as the ordinary way of being saved, and we shouldn’t separate what he joined.”",
 avoid="Do not overstate the textual case for the longer ending’s Marcan authorship; rest on its canonicity and on parallel texts.",
 read=["CCC 1250–1261","Council of Trent, Session 7, canons on baptism (esp. 5, 13)","Acts 2:38–41; 16:15, 33"])

D('matthew',1,25,
 ctx="Matthew 1:18–25 narrates the virginal conception from Joseph’s point of view. The angel tells Joseph in a dream to take Mary as his wife, for ‘that which is conceived in her, is of the Holy Ghost’ (1:20), fulfilling Isaiah 7:14 (‘a virgin shall be with child’, 1:23). ‘And Joseph rising up from sleep, did as the angel of the Lord had commanded him, and took unto him his wife. And he knew her not till she brought forth her firstborn son: and he called his name JESUS’ (1:24–25).",
 who="‘Till’ (heōs) implies that after Jesus’ birth Joseph and Mary had normal marital relations. ‘Firstborn’ implies later children. Combined with the ‘brothers of the Lord’ (Matthew 13:55), this is taken to refute Mary’s perpetual virginity. Note: Luther, Calvin and Zwingli all affirmed Mary’s perpetual virginity; the denial became common among Protestants later.",
 lang="“Till” (heōs hou): ‘until’. In Greek and Hebrew usage, ‘until’ asserts what happened up to a point without implying a change afterwards. The Greek Old Testament: ‘Michol the daughter of Saul had no child to the day of her death’ (2 Samuel 6:23, heōs, ‘until the day of her death’). ‘The Lord said to my Lord: Sit thou at my right hand: until I make thy enemies thy footstool’ (Psalm 109:1; does he leave afterwards?). ‘Behold I am with you all days, even to (heōs) the consummation of the world’ (Matthew 28:20). ‘He must reign, until he hath put all his enemies under his feet’ (1 Corinthians 15:25).\n\n“Firstborn” (prōtotokos): a legal title for the son who ‘opens the womb’ (Exodus 13:2; 34:19), consecrated to God, whether or not other children follow. A Jewish funerary inscription from Tell el-Yehudieh in Egypt (5 BC) speaks of a woman who died ‘in the pains of delivering her firstborn child’: an only child called firstborn.",
 steps=[
  ["Matthew’s point is the virginal conception","Matthew is establishing that Joseph was not Jesus’ biological father, fulfilling Isaiah 7:14. ‘He knew her not until she brought forth her son’ guarantees that Jesus was conceived and born of a virgin. What happened afterwards is outside Matthew’s concern."],
  ["‘Until’ does not imply a change afterwards","See the examples: Michal had no child ‘until’ her death; Christ is with us ‘until’ the end of the world; he reigns ‘until’ all enemies are subdued. In each case ‘until’ marks a point without implying the opposite afterwards."],
  ["‘Firstborn’ is a legal status","Every firstborn son had to be consecrated to God (Exodus 13:2), and the title was given at birth, before anyone could know if more children would follow. Luke uses it in exactly this legal context (Luke 2:7, 22–23)."],
  ["The rest of Scripture is consistent with perpetual virginity","Mary’s question at the Annunciation, ‘How shall this be done, because I know not man?’ (Luke 1:34), suggests to many Fathers an intention of virginity, since a betrothed woman would naturally expect children. Jesus on the Cross entrusts his mother to John (John 19:26–27), which would be strange if she had other sons to care for her. Nowhere is anyone called ‘son of Mary’ except Jesus (Mark 6:3: ‘the carpenter, the son of Mary’)."],
  ["Universal ancient and Reformation witness","The perpetual virginity is affirmed by the Fathers (Athanasius called her ‘Ever-Virgin’, aeiparthenos), the Second Council of Constantinople (553) and the Lateran Synod of 649. Luther, Calvin, Zwingli and the 17th-century Anglican divines accepted it. The denial is mainly a phenomenon of later Protestantism."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Jerome, Against Helvidius 6–8 (c. 383)","Answers Helvidius’ argument from ‘until’ and ‘firstborn’ with the scriptural examples above: ‘until’ often refers to what had not happened, without implying what happened afterwards (paraphrased)."],
  ["Athanasius, Discourses against the Arians II.70 (c. 358)","Calls Mary ‘Ever-Virgin’."],
  ["Second Council of Constantinople (553)","Uses the title ‘ever-virgin Mary’ (aeiparthenos) in its anathemas."],
  ["Martin Luther, Sermon on John 2 (1539)","Christ, our Saviour, was the real and natural fruit of Mary’s virginal womb… without the cooperation of a man, and she remained a virgin after that (paraphrased; Luther’s Works, vol. 22)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Normal reading of ‘until’ in English implies a change.”","In English, often. In biblical Greek and Hebrew, not necessarily, as the examples show. The question is Matthew’s usage, not ours."],
  ["“A marriage without relations isn’t a real marriage.”","Marriage is constituted by consent, not by consummation alone (Thomas, ST III q.29 a.2). Joseph and Mary’s marriage was true, and its purpose was to protect and raise the Son of God."]],
 talk="“‘Until’ in the Bible doesn’t mean things changed afterwards. Second Samuel says Michal had no children ‘until the day of her death’; that doesn’t mean she had some after. Jesus says ‘I am with you until the end of the world’; he doesn’t leave afterwards. Matthew is just making clear Joseph wasn’t Jesus’ father. And ‘firstborn’ was a legal title given at birth. Even Luther and Calvin believed Mary remained a virgin.”",
 avoid="Do not claim the verse proves perpetual virginity; it is compatible with it. The doctrine rests on Tradition, read with Scripture.",
 read=["CCC 496–507","Jerome, Against Helvidius","Lumen Gentium 57","Tim Staples (2014) Behold Your Mother. El Cajon: Catholic Answers, chs. 7–8"])

D('matthew',13,55,
 ctx="Matthew 13:53–58: Jesus teaches in his home town of Nazareth. ‘Is not this the carpenter’s son? Is not his mother called Mary, and his brethren James, and Joseph, and Simon, and Jude: and his sisters, are they not all with us?’ (13:55–56). The parallel in Mark 6:3 reads: ‘Is not this the carpenter, the son of Mary, the brother of James, and Joseph, and Jude, and Simon? are not also his sisters here with us?’",
 who="Mary had other children, Jesus’ younger brothers and sisters, named here. So Mary was not perpetually a virgin.",
 lang="“Brethren” (adelphoi). In Hebrew and Aramaic there is no special word for cousin; ’ach (brother) covers nephews, cousins and kinsmen. The Greek Old Testament keeps this broad usage: Lot, Abraham’s nephew, is called his ‘brother’ (Genesis 13:8; 14:14, 16); Laban calls his nephew Jacob ‘brother’ (Genesis 29:15). In the New Testament, adelphoi is used of 120 disciples (Acts 1:15) and of all Christians.",
 steps=[
  ["‘Brother’ in Scripture has a broad meaning","The Bible routinely calls nephews and other relatives ‘brothers’ (Genesis 13:8; 14:14; 29:15). The ‘brothers of the Lord’ were well-known members of the Jerusalem church (Acts 1:14; Galatians 1:19), and the term identified a family group, not necessarily sons of the same mother."],
  ["James and Joseph were sons of another Mary","At the Cross: ‘Mary Magdalen, and Mary the mother of James and Joseph’ (Matthew 27:56); ‘Mary the mother of James the less and of Joseph’ (Mark 15:40). These are the same names as two of the ‘brethren’ in 13:55. This other Mary is identified as ‘Mary of Cleophas’ (John 19:25). So at least James and Joseph had a different mother."],
  ["Jesus entrusts Mary to John","‘Woman, behold thy son… Behold thy mother. And from that hour, the disciple took her to his own’ (John 19:26–27). Under Jewish custom the next son would care for a widowed mother. Entrusting her to John makes most sense if she had no other sons."],
  ["Only Jesus is called ‘the son of Mary’","Mark 6:3 says ‘the son of Mary’, with the article, and ‘the brother of James’. The brethren are never called sons of Mary or sons of Joseph."],
  ["Two ancient explanations","The Eastern tradition, following the Protoevangelium of James (c. 150), held they were Joseph’s children by an earlier marriage (step-brothers). Jerome (c. 383) argued they were cousins. Both preserve the perpetual virginity; the Church has not defined which is correct."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Protoevangelium of James 9 (c. 150)","Joseph, an older widower with sons, is chosen to take Mary into his care (not Scripture, but an early witness to belief in her virginity)."],
  ["Jerome, Against Helvidius 13–19 (c. 383)","Brethren in Scripture are of four kinds: by nature, by race, by kindred and by affection; the Lord’s brethren were kinsmen, sons of Mary of Cleophas (paraphrased)."],
  ["Hegesippus, cited by Eusebius, Church History III.11 (c. 170)","Simeon, the second bishop of Jerusalem, was a son of Clopas, Joseph’s brother, and a cousin of the Lord (paraphrased)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Greek has a word for cousin (anepsios, Colossians 4:10). Why not use it?”","The Gospel writers kept the family’s traditional title, ‘the brothers of the Lord’, used in the Aramaic-speaking Jerusalem church. The Greek Old Testament likewise keeps ‘brother’ for relatives although Greek had other words."],
  ["“John 7:5 says his brothers did not believe in him. So they weren’t apostles.”","Correct, which also tells against reading them as sons of Mary, the first believer. It fits a wider circle of relatives."]],
 talk="“In the Bible ‘brother’ often means relative: Lot was Abraham’s nephew and he’s called his brother. And look at the Crucifixion: James and Joseph, two of the ‘brothers’ named here, have a different mother, ‘Mary the mother of James and Joseph’. Jesus also gave his mother to John at the Cross, which wouldn’t make sense if she had other sons. That’s why the early Church believed Mary stayed a virgin, and so did Luther and Calvin.”",
 avoid="Do not claim certainty about whether they were cousins or step-brothers; the Church leaves it open.",
 read=["CCC 499–501","Jerome, Against Helvidius","John P. Meier (1991) A Marginal Jew vol. 1. New York: Doubleday, pp. 318–332 (a Catholic scholar who weighs the evidence)"])

D('romans',3,23,
 ctx="Romans 3:9–26: Paul concludes his indictment of Jews and Gentiles: ‘we have charged both Jews, and Greeks, that they are all under sin. As it is written: There is not any man just. There is none that understandeth, there is none that seeketh after God… For all have sinned, and do need the glory of God. Being justified freely by his grace, through the redemption, that is in Christ Jesus’ (3:9–11, 23–24). The quotations come from the Psalms (13:1–3; 5:10; 139:4; 9:28; 35:2 in the Douay) and Isaiah 59:7–8.",
 who="If all have sinned, Mary has sinned. Therefore the Immaculate Conception (that Mary was preserved from original sin) and Mary’s sinlessness contradict Scripture. Mary also calls God ‘my Saviour’ (Luke 1:47), so she needed saving from sin.",
 lang="“All have sinned” (pantes hēmarton): ‘all’ in the context of Jews and Gentiles as groups (3:9, 22: ‘there is no distinction’). The Psalm quoted in 3:10–12 (Psalm 13) is about ‘the fool’ and the wicked, and itself speaks of ‘the just generation’ (13:5 Douay).\n\n“Do need” (hysterountai): fall short of, lack.",
 steps=[
  ["Paul’s point is universal need of grace, Jew and Gentile alike","Romans 1–3 proves that Jews have no advantage over Gentiles in the matter of sin: ‘there is no distinction’ (3:22). ‘All’ is about groups, not a census of every individual."],
  ["‘All’ has obvious exceptions","Jesus never sinned (Hebrews 4:15). Infants and those who never reach the use of reason commit no personal sins. Paul himself says Jacob and Esau, before birth, ‘had not done any good or evil’ (Romans 9:11). So ‘all have sinned’ cannot mean every human being without exception has personally sinned."],
  ["The Psalms quoted distinguish the wicked and the just","Psalm 13 (14) describes the fool who says ‘there is no God’, and continues: ‘God is in the just generation’ (13:5 Douay). Luke calls Zachary and Elizabeth ‘both just before God, walking in all the commandments’ (Luke 1:6). Paul uses the Psalms rhetorically to show universal sinfulness, not to deny any righteous person ever existed."],
  ["Mary was redeemed: preserved rather than cleansed","Pius IX defined (Ineffabilis Deus, 1854) that Mary was preserved from original sin ‘by a singular grace and privilege of almighty God, in view of the merits of Jesus Christ, the Saviour of the human race’. She needed a Saviour and had one; she was redeemed in a more perfect way, by prevention. Analogy (from Duns Scotus’ argument): a man may be saved from a pit by being pulled out after falling in, or by being held back before he falls. Both are rescued; the second more perfectly."],
  ["Scriptural foundations","Luke 1:28: the angel greets her as kecharitōmenē, ‘one who has been graced’ (perfect participle), used as a name. Genesis 3:15 promises enmity between the woman and the serpent. The Fathers called her ‘all-holy’ (panagia) and the new Eve."],
  ["An honest note about Thomas","Thomas Aquinas did not accept that Mary was conceived without original sin; he held that she was sanctified in the womb after conception, though she never committed personal sin (ST III q.27 a.2, a.4). Bernard of Clairvaux also opposed the feast. The decisive argument came from Duns Scotus (d. 1308): preservative redemption. The Church defined it in 1854. Thomas’ concern, that Mary too must be redeemed by Christ, is fully preserved in the definition."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Augustine, On Nature and Grace 36.42 (415)","Except the holy Virgin Mary, about whom, out of honour to the Lord, I wish to raise no question when sin is discussed (paraphrased)."],
  ["Ephrem the Syrian, Carmina Nisibena 27 (c. 370)","‘You alone and your Mother are more beautiful than any others; for there is no blemish in you, nor any stain upon your Mother’ (paraphrased)."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, ST III q.27 a.4","Mary committed no actual sin, mortal or venial."],
  ["Pius IX, Ineffabilis Deus (1854)","Defines the Immaculate Conception, in view of the merits of Jesus Christ, the Saviour."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Luke 1:47: Mary calls God ‘my Saviour’.”","Yes: she was saved, by preservation. The definition itself says ‘in view of the merits of Jesus Christ, the Saviour’. See the note on Luke 1:47."],
  ["“Mary offered a sin offering after childbirth (Luke 2:24).”","The purification offering (Leviticus 12:6–8) was for ritual impurity after childbirth, not for moral sin. Jesus too was presented and circumcised and baptized, though sinless."]],
 talk="“Paul is saying Jews and Gentiles are all in the same boat; nobody gets an advantage. But ‘all’ can’t mean every single person: Jesus never sinned, and babies haven’t committed sins. Catholics believe Mary needed a Saviour, and she was saved in a special way: preserved from sin instead of cleaned up afterwards, through Christ’s merits. It’s like being stopped before falling into a pit, rather than pulled out once you’re in.”",
 avoid="Do not pretend the Immaculate Conception was always held by everyone or by Thomas; be honest about the history and show why the definition answers the earlier concerns.",
 read=["CCC 490–493","Pius IX (1854) Ineffabilis Deus","ST III q.27 (with care)","Tim Staples (2014) Behold Your Mother, chs. 3–5"])

D('1-timothy',2,5,
 ctx="1 Timothy 2:1–6: ‘I desire therefore, first of all, that supplications, prayers, intercessions, and thanksgivings be made for all men: for kings, and for all that are in high station… For this is good and acceptable in the sight of God our Saviour, who will have all men to be saved, and to come to the knowledge of the truth. For there is one God, and one mediator of God and men, the man Christ Jesus: who gave himself a redemption for all.’",
 who="If Christ is the one mediator, Catholics cannot call on Mary (‘Mediatrix’), the saints or priests as mediators. Praying to saints and Mary bypasses or competes with Christ.",
 lang="“Mediator” (mesitēs): go-between, one who stands between two parties to make peace or a covenant. In Hebrews Christ is ‘mediator of a new testament’ (8:6; 9:15; 12:24). Moses is called a mediator of the Law (Galatians 3:19–20).\n\n“Intercessions” (enteuxeis, 2:1): petitions on behalf of others.",
 steps=[
  ["Paul’s first point is that Christians should intercede for others","The verse comes in the middle of an instruction to pray, petition and intercede ‘for all men’ (2:1). Paul sees no conflict between Christ’s unique mediation and Christians interceding for one another; he commands both in the same paragraph. Christian intercession is good ‘because’ there is one mediator who wills all to be saved."],
  ["Christ’s mediation is unique as redeemer","‘One mediator… who gave himself a redemption for all’ (2:6). Only Christ, God and man, reconciles humanity to God by his sacrifice. No saint, not even Mary, adds to this. Catholics affirm it without reservation (CCC 618; Lumen Gentium 60)."],
  ["Others share in Christ’s mediation subordinately","Vatican II: ‘The unique mediation of the Redeemer does not exclude but rather gives rise to a manifold cooperation which is but a sharing in this one source’ (LG 62). Moses interceded for Israel (Exodus 32:11–14); Job for his friends (Job 42:8); Paul asks others to pray for him (Romans 15:30). ‘The continual prayer of a just man availeth much’ (James 5:16)."],
  ["The saints in heaven are not cut off from the Body","Christ has one Body, which death does not divide (Romans 8:38; 14:8). God is ‘not the God of the dead, but of the living: for all live to him’ (Luke 20:38). The saints pray: ‘the prayers of saints’ rise before God (Revelation 5:8; 8:3–4); the martyrs cry out to God about events on earth (6:9–10). Asking them to pray for us is like asking a fellow Christian on earth."],
  ["Mary’s mediation is maternal intercession","‘Mediatrix’ is a title used in Lumen Gentium 62 with the explanation that it ‘neither takes away from nor adds anything to the dignity and efficaciousness of Christ the one Mediator’. Mary’s role is that of a mother interceding, as at Cana (John 2:3)."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Origen, On Prayer 11 (c. 233)","The saints who have died pray along with us (paraphrased)."],
  ["Inscriptions in the Roman catacombs (3rd–4th centuries)","Many epitaphs ask the departed to pray for the living, e.g. ‘pray for us’ (paraphrased examples recorded by archaeologists)."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, ST III q.26 a.1","Christ alone is the perfect mediator; others may be called mediators ministerially, as preparing or serving union with God."],
  ["Lumen Gentium 60–62 (1964)","Mary’s maternal role flows from Christ’s mediation and depends entirely on it."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Asking a living friend to pray is fine; asking the dead is necromancy.”","Necromancy is summoning the dead for knowledge (see Deuteronomy 18:11). The saints are alive in Christ (Luke 20:38) and pray already (Revelation 5:8). Asking for prayer is not consultation of the dead."],
  ["“How can saints hear millions of prayers?”","By God’s power, as they see in God (1 Corinthians 13:12). Revelation shows the elders presenting the prayers of saints, and the angels in heaven rejoice at a sinner’s repentance on earth (Luke 15:10), so heaven knows what happens on earth."]],
 talk="“Look at the verses just before: Paul tells us to intercede for everyone, because there’s one mediator. So Christian intercession isn’t competition with Christ; it flows from him. Christ alone redeemed us. When I ask my mum, my pastor, or a saint in heaven to pray for me, I’m not getting a different mediator; I’m asking members of Christ’s body to pray with me. Jesus said God is the God of the living, for all live to him.”",
 avoid="Do not use ‘Co-Redemptrix’ casually; the term is not defined and was discouraged in the 2025 doctrinal note Mater Populi Fidelis. Speak of Mary’s intercession.",
 read=["Lumen Gentium 60–62","CCC 956, 969–970, 2683","ST III q.26","Revelation 5:8; 8:3–4"])

D('deuteronomy',18,11,
 ctx="Deuteronomy 18:9–14 forbids Israel to imitate the Canaanites: ‘Neither let there be found among you any one that shall expiate his son or daughter, making them to pass through the fire: or that consulteth soothsayers, or observeth dreams and omens, neither let there be any wizard, nor charmer, nor any one that consulteth pythonic spirits, or fortune tellers, or that seeketh the truth from the dead’ (18:10–11). Then God promises a true prophet like Moses to whom Israel must listen (18:15–18).",
 who="Praying to saints is ‘consulting the dead’, which God condemns as an abomination. King Saul’s visit to the witch of Endor (1 Samuel 28) is cited as an example.",
 lang="“Seeketh the truth from the dead” (Hebrew doresh el-hammetim): inquiring of the dead, seeking information or oracles. It belongs with divination, sorcery and mediums: techniques for gaining hidden knowledge or power apart from God.",
 steps=[
  ["The prohibition is about divination and conjuring","The list is all occult practice: child sacrifice, soothsaying, omens, charms, mediums, necromancy. The purpose is to gain secret knowledge or control apart from God, instead of listening to his prophet (18:14–15). The Catholic Church condemns all of this (CCC 2115–2117: ‘all practices of magic or sorcery… recourse to mediums’)."],
  ["Asking for prayer is not seeking information","Catholics do not summon the saints, ask them to reveal the future, or seek messages from them. We ask them to pray to God for us, just as we ask fellow Christians. That is an act of charity in the communion of saints, directed to God."],
  ["The saints are alive in Christ","Jesus: ‘he is not the God of the dead, but of the living: for all live to him’ (Luke 20:38). Moses and Elijah talk with Christ at the Transfiguration (Matthew 17:3). The souls under the altar speak to God about events on earth (Revelation 6:9–10). Hebrews 12:1: ‘a cloud of witnesses’ surrounds us."],
  ["Scripture shows the departed praying for the living","2 Maccabees 15:12–14: Judas sees in a vision the dead high priest Onias and the prophet Jeremiah praying for the people: ‘This is a lover of his brethren, and of the people of Israel: this is he that prayeth much for the people, and for all the holy city, Jeremias the prophet of God.’ Revelation 5:8: the elders offer ‘the prayers of saints’."],
  ["Saul and the witch of Endor","Saul consulted a medium to call up Samuel for knowledge of the battle’s outcome after God had refused to answer him (1 Samuel 28:6–7). That is exactly divination through the dead. Catholics do nothing of the kind."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Cyril of Jerusalem, Mystagogical Catecheses 5.9 (c. 350)","In the Eucharistic prayer we commemorate patriarchs, prophets, apostles and martyrs, that at their prayers and intercessions God would receive our petition (paraphrased)."],
  ["Jerome, Against Vigilantius 6 (406)","If the apostles and martyrs, while still in the body, could pray for others, how much more after their crowns and victories (paraphrased)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Any communication with the dead is forbidden.”","Then Jesus broke the law at the Transfiguration. The forbidden thing is occult practice, not the communion of saints."]],
 talk="“That verse is about divination: mediums, séances and trying to get secret knowledge from the dead, like Saul with the witch of Endor. The Church condemns all that. Asking a saint to pray for you is completely different: you’re not trying to get information or power, you’re asking a fellow Christian who is alive in Christ to pray to God with you. Jesus said God is the God of the living, and Revelation shows the saints in heaven offering prayers.”",
 avoid="Do not dismiss the danger of the occult; the Church takes it seriously (CCC 2116–2117).",
 read=["CCC 956, 2115–2117, 2683","2 Maccabees 15:12–16","Revelation 5:8; 6:9–11"])

D('exodus',20,4,
 ctx="Exodus 20:2–6: ‘I am the Lord thy God… Thou shalt not have strange gods before me. Thou shalt not make to thyself a graven thing, nor the likeness of any thing that is in heaven above, or in the earth beneath, nor of those things that are in the waters under the earth. Thou shalt not adore them, nor serve them: I am the Lord thy God, mighty, jealous.’ Five chapters later, God commands: ‘Thou shalt make also two cherubims of beaten gold, on the two sides of the oracle’ (25:18).",
 who="The second commandment (in the Reformed numbering) forbids making images for religious use. Catholic statues, crucifixes, icons, and kneeling or praying before them, are idolatry. Some say Catholics ‘removed’ this commandment by combining it with the first and splitting the last.",
 lang="“Graven thing” (pesel): a carved image, specifically an idol. “Adore” (tishtachaweh, bow down) and “serve” (ta‘ovdem, worship): the prohibition targets images made for worship as gods.\n\nNumbering: the Ten Commandments are not numbered in the text; Jews, Catholics, Lutherans, Orthodox and Reformed divide them differently. Augustine’s division, used by Catholics and Lutherans, treats verses 3–6 as one commandment and ‘covet’ as two (CCC 2066). The Catholic Church has never removed any words from Exodus 20.",
 steps=[
  ["The commandment forbids idols","‘Thou shalt not adore them, nor serve them.’ The prohibition is of images made to be worshipped as gods, or used to represent the invisible God as if he could be captured in a statue (Deuteronomy 4:15–19: ‘you saw not any similitude in the day that the Lord spoke to you in Horeb’)."],
  ["God himself commanded religious images","The golden cherubim on the ark (Exodus 25:18–22), embroidered cherubim on the tabernacle curtains (26:1), carved cherubim, palms and flowers on the Temple walls and doors (1 Kings 6:23–35), twelve bronze oxen holding the sea (1 Kings 7:25), and the bronze serpent, a healing image Israel looked at (Numbers 21:8–9), which Jesus applies to himself (John 3:14)."],
  ["Misuse makes an image an idol","When Israel later burned incense to the bronze serpent, Hezekiah destroyed it (2 Kings 18:4). The same object God commanded became an idol when worshipped. Catholic teaching draws exactly this line."],
  ["The Incarnation changes everything","In the Old Testament God had not shown his face. ‘The Word was made flesh’ (John 1:14); ‘he that seeth me seeth the Father’ (14:9); Christ is ‘the image (eikōn) of the invisible God’ (Colossians 1:15). Since God made himself visible, we may depict what he showed us. This was the argument of John Damascene and the Second Council of Nicaea (787)."],
  ["Honour passes to the person represented","Nicaea II: ‘the honour paid to an image passes to its prototype’ (quoting Basil, On the Holy Spirit 18.45). Kneeling before a crucifix is like kissing a photograph of your mother; the love is directed to the person, not to paper. Catholics give latria (adoration) to God alone, and relative honour to images (CCC 2132; ST II-II q.81 a.3 ad 3)."]],
 fathers=[
  ["John of Damascus, On the Divine Images I.16 (c. 730)","I do not worship matter; I worship the Creator of matter who became matter for my sake… and who worked out my salvation through matter (paraphrased)."],
  ["Second Council of Nicaea (787)","Images of Christ, Mary, angels and saints may be set up and given veneration of honour, not true worship (latria), which belongs to God alone."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, ST III q.25 a.3","The movement of the mind toward an image as an image passes to the thing represented."],
  ["Archaeology: Dura-Europos (c. 235) and the catacombs","Early Christian house-church and catacomb paintings depict Christ as the Good Shepherd, biblical scenes and saints."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Catholics bow and kneel before statues. That is ‘bowing down’.”","Bowing is a gesture whose meaning depends on intention: Abraham bowed to the Hittites (Genesis 23:7), Solomon bowed to his mother (1 Kings 2:19), and Israel bowed before the ark. Catholics bow before images of Christ to honour Christ."],
  ["“Catholics deleted the second commandment.”","Every Catholic Bible contains Exodus 20:4–6 in full. Catechisms use a short summary formula; Augustine’s division into ten predates the Reformation by a millennium. Lutherans use it too."]],
 talk="“The commandment says don’t make idols to bow down and worship. But five chapters later God tells Moses to make golden cherubim for the ark, and he told him to make a bronze serpent people looked at to be healed; Jesus compares himself to it. What God forbids is worshipping images as gods. Catholics worship God alone. A crucifix is like a photo of someone you love: the honour goes to the person. And since God became man and showed us his face, we can paint it.”",
 avoid="Do not deny that superstitious use of images can happen; the Church warns against it (CCC 2111).",
 read=["CCC 1159–1162, 2129–2132","Second Council of Nicaea (787)","John of Damascus, Three Treatises on the Divine Images","ST II-II q.81 a.3 ad 3; III q.25 a.3"])

D('matthew',6,7,
 ctx="Matthew 6:5–15, in the Sermon on the Mount. Jesus warns against praying to be seen (6:5–6). ‘And when you are praying, speak not much, as the heathens. For they think that in their much speaking they may be heard. Be not you therefore like to them, for your Father knoweth what is needful for you, before you ask him. Thus therefore shall you pray: Our Father who art in heaven…’ (6:7–9).",
 who="The Rosary, litanies, the Jesus Prayer and other repetitive prayers are the ‘vain repetitions’ (KJV) Jesus forbade.",
 lang="“Speak not much” (mē battalogēsēte): ‘do not babble’. The verb battalogeō is rare, probably imitating meaningless sound (‘batta-batta’). The fault is explained: they think they will be heard ‘for their much speaking’ (polylogia), as if words could wear the gods down or bind them by formula. The KJV ‘vain repetitions’ is an interpretation.",
 steps=[
  ["Jesus forbids babbling and manipulation, not repetition","Pagan prayer often piled up divine names and formulas to secure a god’s attention or force a result. Jesus’ objection is the motive: thinking you will be heard because of the quantity of words. The cure is trust: ‘your Father knoweth’."],
  ["Jesus himself repeated his prayer","In Gethsemane: ‘he went again: and he prayed the third time, saying the selfsame word’ (Matthew 26:44). In the parable of the importunate widow, he praises persistence (Luke 18:1–8)."],
  ["Scripture is full of repetition in prayer","Psalm 135 (136) repeats ‘for his mercy endureth for ever’ 26 times. In heaven the four living creatures ‘rested not day and night, saying: Holy, holy, holy, Lord God Almighty’ (Revelation 4:8). The tax collector repeats ‘O God, be merciful to me a sinner’ (Luke 18:13)."],
  ["The Rosary is meditation on the Gospel","The Hail Mary is drawn from Luke 1:28 and 1:42; the Our Father from Matthew 6:9–13. The repetition is the background for meditating on twenty mysteries of Christ’s life (John Paul II, Rosarium Virginis Mariae, 2002). Many Protestants likewise repeat choruses in worship songs."],
  ["The right test is the heart","Any prayer, repeated or spontaneous, can become empty if the heart is absent. Catholic teaching agrees: vocal prayer must be attentive (CCC 2700–2704)."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Augustine, Letter 130 to Proba, 10.20 (412)","To pray much is not to babble much; long prayer is not condemned, but many words with an idle heart (paraphrased)."],
  ["Cassian, Conferences 10.10 (c. 425)","Recommends constant repetition of ‘O God, come to my assistance; O Lord, make haste to help me’ (Psalm 69:2) as a way of unceasing prayer."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Fifty Hail Marys is exactly ‘much speaking’.”","Only if one thinks one is heard because of the number. The Rosary is a meditative prayer, like the 26 refrains of Psalm 136 or the angels’ unending Sanctus."]],
 talk="“Jesus is warning against pagan babbling, piling up words thinking that more words force God to listen. But Jesus himself prayed the same words three times in Gethsemane, Psalm 136 repeats ‘his mercy endures forever’ 26 times, and the angels in heaven never stop saying ‘Holy, holy, holy’. The Rosary is meditating on Jesus’ life while praying words taken straight from the Gospels. It’s only vain if your heart isn’t in it, and that’s true of any prayer.”",
 avoid="Do not deny that rote prayer can become mechanical; encourage attentive prayer.",
 read=["CCC 2700–2704, 2708, 2678","John Paul II (2002) Rosarium Virginis Mariae","Augustine, Letter 130"])

D('2-corinthians',5,8,
 ctx="2 Corinthians 4:16–5:10. Paul contrasts the outward man decaying and the inward renewed, ‘our earthly house of this habitation’ and ‘a building of God… eternal in heaven’ (5:1). ‘Therefore having always confidence, knowing that, while we are in the body, we are absent from the Lord… we are confident, and have a good will to be absent rather from the body, and to be present with the Lord. And therefore we labour, whether absent or present, to please him. For we must all be manifested before the judgment seat of Christ, that every one may receive the proper things of the body, according as he hath done, whether it be good or evil’ (5:6–10).",
 who="To be absent from the body is to be present with the Lord, immediately. There is no intermediate state of purification; therefore no purgatory.",
 lang="“Absent” (ekdēmēsai) and “present” (endēmēsai): to be away from home and at home. Paul expresses a preference (eudokoumen, ‘we prefer’), not a theological timetable for every believer.",
 steps=[
  ["Paul states his longing, not a rule for all","Paul says ‘we would rather’ be away from the body and at home with the Lord. He does not say every Christian goes instantly into the full vision of God at death. Catholics share his hope: those who die perfectly purified, like the martyrs, are at once with the Lord."],
  ["Purgatory is ‘with the Lord’, not away from him","Purgatory is not a place of separation from Christ; it is the final purification of those who die in his grace and friendship (CCC 1030). The souls there are saved, belong to him and are being prepared for the vision of God. Paul’s contrast is between earthly life and being with the Lord, and purgatory falls on the ‘with the Lord’ side."],
  ["Paul himself immediately mentions judgment by works","‘We must all be manifested before the judgment seat of Christ… according as he hath done, whether it be good or evil’ (5:10). Elsewhere he describes Christians whose works are burned at the judgment while they themselves are saved ‘yet so as by fire’ (1 Corinthians 3:13–15)."],
  ["Nothing unclean enters heaven","‘There shall not enter into it any thing defiled’ (Revelation 21:27). ‘Without holiness no man shall see God’ (Hebrews 12:14). Most Christians die forgiven but still attached to sin. God completes his work in them (Philippians 1:6)."],
  ["Prayer for the dead is ancient","2 Maccabees 12:43–46: Judas sends money for a sacrifice for the dead, ‘that they may be loosed from sins’. Paul prays for Onesiphorus, apparently dead: ‘The Lord grant unto him to find mercy of the Lord in that day’ (2 Timothy 1:18). Early Christian epitaphs and Tertullian (On the Crown 3, c. 211) attest offerings for the dead on their anniversaries."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Tertullian, On Monogamy 10 (c. 213)","A widow prays for her husband’s soul, asks refreshment for him and offers on the anniversary of his death (paraphrased)."],
  ["Augustine, Confessions IX.13 and City of God XXI.13, 24 (c. 400–420)","Prays for his mother Monica; some suffer temporal punishments after death before the last judgment (paraphrased)."],
  ["Council of Florence (1439); Trent, Session 25 (1563)","Purgatory exists and the souls detained there are helped by the prayers of the faithful and above all by the Mass."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Purgatory means Christ’s sacrifice wasn’t enough.”","Purgatory is Christ’s sacrifice completing its work in us, not a supplement to it. See the note on John 19:30."],
  ["“The thief went straight to paradise.”","Some souls need no purification. See the note on Luke 23:43."]],
 talk="“Paul is saying he’d rather be with the Lord than stay in this body, and so would I. Purgatory isn’t being away from the Lord; it’s being with him, saved, and being made ready to see him face to face, because nothing unclean enters heaven. Paul says in the next verses that we’ll all stand before Christ’s judgment for what we’ve done, and in 1 Corinthians 3 he describes someone saved ‘but only as through fire’. That’s purgatory.”",
 avoid="Do not describe purgatory as a ‘second chance’ or as a lesser hell; it is purification of the saved.",
 read=["CCC 1030–1032, 1472","Benedict XVI (2007) Spe Salvi 45–48","1 Corinthians 3:10–15; 2 Maccabees 12:38–46"])

# ---------------- New entries ----------------
add('luke',1,47,'Luke 1:47','"My spirit hath rejoiced in God my Saviour"',
 'Mary calls God her Saviour, so she was a sinner who needed saving, and the Immaculate Conception is false.',
 ['Catholic teaching says the same: Mary needed a Saviour and was saved by Christ. The definition says she was preserved from sin "in view of the merits of Jesus Christ, the Saviour of the human race" (1854).',
  'She was redeemed in a more perfect way: preserved from falling rather than lifted up after falling.',
  'God saves from many things: sin, danger, death. "God my Saviour" is common Old Testament language (Psalm 24:5; Isaiah 12:2; Habakkuk 3:18, which Mary echoes).',
  'Mary rejoices because "he that is mighty hath done great things to me" (1:49), which fits a singular grace.'],
 'Mary had a Saviour: Christ saved her first and most perfectly, by preserving her from all sin.',
 'CCC 491–492; Ineffabilis Deus (1854)',
 disp='d-luke1-28',tags=('mary',))
D('luke',1,47,
 ctx="After the Visitation, Elizabeth greets Mary: ‘Blessed art thou among women… blessed art thou that hast believed’ (1:42, 45). Mary responds with the Magnificat: ‘My soul doth magnify the Lord. And my spirit hath rejoiced in God my Saviour. Because he hath regarded the humility of his handmaid; for behold from henceforth all generations shall call me blessed. Because he that is mighty hath done great things to me; and holy is his name’ (1:46–49). The canticle is woven from Old Testament texts, especially Hannah’s song (1 Samuel 2:1–10).",
 who="Only sinners need a Saviour. Mary’s words prove she knew herself to be a sinner, contradicting the Catholic doctrine that she was conceived without original sin and never sinned.",
 lang="“God my Saviour” (tō theō tō sōtēri mou) echoes Habakkuk 3:18 (‘I will joy in God my Jesus’, in the Hebrew ‘the God of my salvation’) and Hannah’s ‘I have joyed in thy salvation’ (1 Samuel 2:1). ‘Saviour’ in the Old Testament is used of God delivering from enemies, danger and death as well as sin.",
 steps=[
  ["The Church teaches Mary was saved by Christ","Ineffabilis Deus (1854): Mary, ‘in the first instance of her conception, by a singular grace and privilege granted by almighty God, in view of the merits of Jesus Christ, the Saviour of the human race, was preserved free from all stain of original sin’. The doctrine affirms that Mary was redeemed. Her ‘God my Saviour’ is the Immaculate Conception in her own words."],
  ["Preservation is a more perfect redemption","Scotus’ argument: it is more perfect to preserve someone from a debt than to pay it after it is incurred. Christ, the perfect Redeemer, would redeem his mother in the most perfect way. CCC 492: Mary is ‘redeemed in a more exalted fashion, by reason of the merits of her Son’."],
  ["‘He hath done great things to me’","Mary herself attributes her privileges to God’s power. Everything she is comes from grace (Luke 1:28: ‘full of grace’, kecharitōmenē). Her humility (‘the humility of his handmaid’) is not a confession of sin but of creaturely lowliness."],
  ["Old Testament language of salvation","Hannah, David and Habakkuk call God their Saviour for deliverance from enemies and trouble. Mary sings as daughter of Zion for Israel’s salvation (1:54–55: ‘He hath received Israel his servant’)."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Pius IX, Ineffabilis Deus (1854)","Preserved ‘in view of the merits of Jesus Christ, the Saviour of the human race’."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, ST III q.27 a.2 ad 2","Thomas held that Mary needed redemption and therefore contracted original sin; the later definition retains his principle (she needed Christ) while teaching preservation. An honest example of doctrine developing."],
  ["Bede, Homily on the Visitation (8th century)","Mary rejoices in the Saviour she bears, who is the source of her own salvation (paraphrased)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Saved from what, if she had no sin?”","From the original sin she would have contracted as a daughter of Adam, had she not been preserved; and from all the evils that come from sin. A child pulled back from a cliff edge has been saved even though he never fell."]],
 talk="“Catholics agree Mary needed a Saviour. The definition of the Immaculate Conception literally says she was preserved from sin ‘in view of the merits of Jesus Christ, the Saviour’. Think of it this way: you can save someone by pulling them out of a pit or by stopping them before they fall in. Both are saved; the second is saved better. Mary was saved first and best, and she sings ‘God my Saviour’ because she knew it.”",
 avoid="Do not claim Thomas taught the Immaculate Conception; he did not. Use him for the principle that Mary needed redemption.",
 read=["CCC 490–493","Pius IX (1854) Ineffabilis Deus","Lumen Gentium 53, 56"])

add('luke',11,28,'Luke 11:28','"Yea rather, blessed are they who hear the word of God"',
 'When a woman blessed Jesus\' mother, Jesus corrected her: the truly blessed are those who hear and keep God\'s word. So Catholics are wrong to honour Mary so highly.',
 ['The Greek menoun can mean "yes indeed" as well as "rather". Jesus does not deny that his mother is blessed; he points to the deeper reason.',
  'Mary is the supreme example of one who hears and keeps God\'s word: "Be it done to me according to thy word" (1:38); "blessed art thou that hast believed" (1:45); she "kept all these words, pondering them in her heart" (2:19, 51).',
  'Luke himself records Mary\'s prophecy: "all generations shall call me blessed" (1:48).',
  'Catholics honour Mary first of all for her faith and obedience, exactly as this verse teaches.'],
 'Mary is blessed twice: as mother of the Lord, and above all as the first and most faithful hearer of the word.',
 'CCC 148–149, 2617–2619, 971; Lumen Gentium 58',
 tags=('mary',))
D('luke',11,28,
 ctx="Luke 11:27–28: ‘And it came to pass, as he spoke these things, a certain woman from the crowd, lifting up her voice, said to him: Blessed is the womb that bore thee, and the paps that gave thee suck. But he said: Yea rather, blessed are they who hear the word of God, and keep it.’ It follows a dispute in which Jesus is accused of casting out devils by Beelzebub (11:14–26).",
 who="Jesus deflects honour from Mary to obedient believers in general. Catholic devotion to Mary, which focuses on her motherhood, goes against Jesus’ own correction.",
 lang="“Yea rather” (menoun): a particle that can correct (‘rather’), affirm and intensify (‘yes indeed’), or both (‘yes, and more than that’). In Romans 10:18 menounge means ‘yes, verily’. The Douay ‘yea rather’ captures both.",
 steps=[
  ["Jesus does not deny his mother is blessed","He does not say ‘No, she is not blessed’. He points to the deeper blessedness: hearing and keeping God’s word. Since Luke has already shown Mary doing exactly that, the saying praises her more, not less."],
  ["Luke presents Mary as the model hearer of the word","‘Behold the handmaid of the Lord; be it done to me according to thy word’ (1:38). Elizabeth: ‘blessed art thou that hast believed, because those things shall be accomplished that were spoken to thee by the Lord’ (1:45). ‘Mary kept all these words, pondering them in her heart’ (2:19, 51). And in Acts she is among the praying disciples awaiting the Spirit (1:14)."],
  ["Mary predicted her own honour","‘For behold from henceforth all generations shall call me blessed’ (1:48). Catholics fulfil this verse."],
  ["The Fathers saw both blessings in Mary","Augustine: Mary is more blessed in receiving the faith of Christ than in conceiving the flesh of Christ (On Holy Virginity 3). This is the Catholic emphasis: her faith comes first."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Augustine, On Holy Virginity 3 (401)","‘More blessed was Mary in receiving the faith of Christ than in conceiving the flesh of Christ’ (paraphrased)."],
  ["Bede, Commentary on Luke, on 11:28 (8th century)","The Lord shows that his mother is blessed not only because she bore the Word in the flesh, but because she kept it in her heart (paraphrased)."],
  ["Lumen Gentium 58 (1964)","Jesus declared blessed those who hear and keep the word, ‘as she was faithfully doing’."]],
 obj=[
  ["“If Mary were so special, Jesus would have agreed with the woman.”","He did agree, and deepened it. Notice he does not rebuke the woman. He teaches the crowd that what made his mother blessed is open to all who hear and keep the word, and she did so first."]],
 talk="“Jesus doesn’t say his mother isn’t blessed. The word can mean ‘yes, and even more’. He says the deeper blessing is hearing God’s word and keeping it. And who did that better than Mary? ‘Let it be done to me according to your word.’ Elizabeth called her blessed because she believed. Luke tells us twice she kept God’s words in her heart. That’s why Catholics honour her: first for her faith.”",
 avoid="Do not ignore the real point of the verse, the priority of faith and obedience; Catholics agree with it.",
 read=["Lumen Gentium 55–59","CCC 148–149, 494, 2617","John Paul II (1987) Redemptoris Mater 12–20"])

add('mark',3,33,'Mark 3:33','"Who is my mother and my brethren?"',
 'When his mother and brothers came looking for him, Jesus pointed to his disciples instead. This shows he did not give his mother special honour, and that Catholics over-exalt her.',
 ['Jesus\' answer: "whosoever shall do the will of God, he is my brother, and my sister, and mother" (3:35). He widens his family to all who do God\'s will, without disowning Mary.',
  'Mary is the first to do God\'s will: "Be it done to me according to thy word" (Luke 1:38). She belongs to Jesus\' family twice over.',
  'Mark 3:21 ("his friends went out to lay hold on him, for they said: He is become mad") does not say Mary thought him mad; "his own" (hoi par\' autou) is a general term for his relatives or associates.',
  'Jesus honoured his mother to the last: from the Cross he entrusted her to the beloved disciple (John 19:26–27).'],
 'Jesus founds a new family of faith, and his mother is its first member.',
 'Lumen Gentium 58; CCC 2617–2619',
 tags=('mary',))
D('mark',3,33,
 ctx="Mark 3:20–35. Crowds press around Jesus so that he cannot eat. ‘And when his friends had heard of it, they went out to lay hold on him. For they said: He is become mad’ (3:21). Scribes from Jerusalem say he casts out devils by Beelzebub (3:22–30). ‘And his mother and his brethren came; and standing without, sent unto him, calling him… And answering them, he said: Who is my mother and my brethren? And looking round about on them who sat about him, he saith: Behold my mother and my brethren. For whosoever shall do the will of God, he is my brother, and my sister, and mother’ (3:31–35).",
 who="This scene is used to show Jesus distancing himself from Mary, and sometimes to claim that Mary thought Jesus was mad (3:21), so she cannot be the sinless, faithful woman Catholics venerate.",
 lang="“His friends” (hoi par’ autou): literally ‘those from him’, his people, relatives or associates. It does not name Mary. The subject of ‘they said’ may even be indefinite (‘people were saying’).",
 steps=[
  ["Jesus opens his family; he does not reject his mother","His point is that discipleship creates bonds as close as family. ‘Whosoever shall do the will of God’ belongs to his family. Mary, who did God’s will perfectly, is therefore doubly his mother: by nature and by faith."],
  ["Mary is not said to think him mad","3:21 refers to ‘his own’ (relatives or associates) and does not mention Mary. In 3:31 Mary comes with the brethren, plausibly out of concern for his safety given the hostility of the scribes. Nothing in Mark attributes unbelief to her."],
  ["Luke’s parallel highlights Mary’s faith","Luke places this saying (8:19–21) after the parable of the sower and phrases it: ‘My mother and my brethren are they who hear the word of God, and do it.’ Luke has already shown Mary as the one who hears and keeps the word (1:38, 45; 2:19, 51)."],
  ["Jesus’ care for his mother continues to the Cross","John 19:25–27: Mary stands at the Cross; Jesus entrusts her to the disciple and the disciple to her."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Augustine, On Holy Virginity 3 (401)","Mary did the will of the Father, and so it is more for her to have been Christ’s disciple than his mother (paraphrased)."],
  ["Lumen Gentium 58 (1964)","In the course of her Son’s preaching she received his words, ‘advanced in her pilgrimage of faith’."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Jesus deliberately didn’t go out to see her.”","He used the moment to teach; we are not told he refused to see her afterwards. Teaching through a family occasion is not a snub."]],
 talk="“Jesus isn’t rejecting his mother; he’s widening his family: whoever does God’s will is his brother, sister and mother. And nobody did God’s will like Mary: ‘Let it be done to me according to your word.’ She’s his mother twice over. And the verse about people thinking he was mad doesn’t mention Mary at all.”",
 avoid="Do not overstate what Mary knew or understood at each moment; Luke says she pondered and did not always understand (2:50).",
 read=["Lumen Gentium 55–59","CCC 2617–2619","John Paul II (1987) Redemptoris Mater"])

add('john',2,4,'John 2:4','"Woman, what is that to me and to thee?"',
 'Jesus addressed his mother coldly as "Woman" and rebuffed her request at Cana. This shows he did not want her interceding, against Catholic belief in Mary\'s intercession.',
 ['"Woman" (gynai) was a respectful address; Jesus uses it from the Cross when lovingly entrusting her to John (19:26).',
  '"What is that to me and to thee" is a Semitic idiom whose tone depends on context; it can mean "how does this concern us?" without rudeness.',
  'Mary\'s response shows she did not hear a refusal: she tells the servants "Whatsoever he shall say to you, do ye" (2:5).',
  'Jesus then does what she asked: his first sign, at her intercession, and "his disciples believed in him" (2:11).'],
 'At Cana Mary intercedes, points to Christ, and Jesus works his first sign at her request.',
 'CCC 2618; Lumen Gentium 58',
 tags=('mary','saints'))
D('john',2,4,
 ctx="John 2:1–11: a wedding at Cana. ‘And the wine failing, the mother of Jesus saith to him: They have no wine. And Jesus saith to her: Woman, what is that to me and to thee? my hour is not yet come. His mother saith to the waiters: Whatsoever he shall say to you, do ye.’ Jesus has six stone jars filled with water and turns it into the best wine. ‘This beginning of miracles did Jesus in Cana of Galilee; and manifested his glory, and his disciples believed in him’ (2:3–5, 11).",
 who="Jesus rebukes Mary (‘Woman, what have I to do with you?’) for intruding, showing that she has no special role in his work and that we should not seek her intercession.",
 lang="“Woman” (gynai): Jesus addresses the Samaritan woman (4:21), Mary Magdalene (20:15) and his mother on the Cross (19:26) this way. Ancient writers used it as a polite and even honorific address.\n\n“What is that to me and to thee?” (ti emoi kai soi): a Hebrew idiom (mah-li walakh) used in the Old Testament with varying tones: Judges 11:12; 2 Samuel 16:10; 1 Kings 17:18; 2 Kings 3:13. It can express hostility or simply mean ‘that is not our concern’ or ‘leave it to me’.\n\n“My hour” in John is the hour of his Passion and glorification (7:30; 12:23; 13:1; 17:1).",
 steps=[
  ["‘Woman’ is not a rebuke","Jesus uses the same word on the Cross in his most tender act toward her (19:26). John uses it, in both places, to evoke ‘the woman’ of Genesis 3:15, whose offspring crushes the serpent. Mary appears in John’s Gospel only at Cana and at the Cross, the beginning and end of Jesus’ signs, both times called ‘Woman’."],
  ["Mary did not take it as a refusal","Her next words are: ‘Whatsoever he shall say to you, do ye.’ She confidently instructs the servants, which she would not do if she had been rebuffed."],
  ["Jesus grants her request","He provides the wine, about 120 to 180 gallons of the best wine. His first sign is worked at his mother’s request, and it leads his disciples to faith. This is the Catholic model of Marian intercession: Mary notices a need, brings it to Jesus, and points people to him."],
  ["‘My hour’ links Cana to the Cross","By acting at Cana, Jesus begins the path to his ‘hour’. At the Cross, when the hour has come, Mary is there again, and he gives her as mother to the disciple. Her role as intercessor begins at Cana and is confirmed at Calvary."],
  ["Mary’s last recorded words","‘Whatsoever he shall say to you, do ye’ are the last words of Mary recorded in the Gospels. Her whole intercession points away from herself to obedience to Christ."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on John, ch. 2, lect. 1","Mary’s request shows her compassion and confidence; Christ’s answer does not refuse but teaches that the miracle comes from his divinity, not from what he received from her (paraphrased)."],
  ["John Paul II, Redemptoris Mater 21 (1987)","At Cana Mary puts herself between her Son and mankind in their needs; she acts as a mediator, not as an outsider, but in her position as mother (paraphrased)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“The idiom always means ‘leave me alone’.”","Not always. In 2 Kings 3:13 and 2 Samuel 16:10 it marks disagreement but not contempt. And here the outcome decides: Jesus does what she asked."]],
 talk="“‘Woman’ wasn’t rude; Jesus calls her the same thing from the Cross when he gives her to John as his mother. And watch what happens: Mary tells the servants ‘do whatever he tells you’, and Jesus does exactly what she asked, his very first miracle. That’s what Catholics mean by Mary’s intercession: she notices our needs, brings them to Jesus, and tells us to do what he says.”",
 avoid="Do not claim Mary forced Jesus’ hand; he acts freely. Emphasize her role as pointing to Christ.",
 read=["CCC 2618, 726","Lumen Gentium 58","John Paul II (1987) Redemptoris Mater 21–22"])

add('acts',4,12,'Acts 4:12','"There is no other name… whereby we must be saved"',
 'Salvation is in Jesus\' name alone. So praying to Mary and the saints, using sacramentals, or seeking their help gives credit to "other names".',
 ['Catholics believe exactly this: salvation is only in Jesus. The saints are not saviours; they are saved by him.',
  'The saints\' prayers are made "through Christ our Lord", in his name. Asking them to pray for us is asking them to pray in Jesus\' name.',
  'Peter speaks these words right after healing a lame man "in the name of Jesus Christ" (4:10) through his own apostolic ministry: Christ saves through his servants without sharing his name with them.',
  'In context Peter is answering the Sanhedrin, which rejected Jesus: no other Messiah, no other covenant, saves.'],
 'One name saves: Jesus. Every Christian, in heaven or on earth, prays and works in that name.',
 'CCC 432, 452, 846, 2666–2668',
 disp='d-1tim2-5',tags=('saints',))
D('acts',4,12,
 ctx="Acts 3–4: Peter and John heal a man lame from birth at the Temple gate ‘in the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth’ (3:6). Arrested and brought before the Sanhedrin, Peter, ‘filled with the Holy Ghost’, declares: ‘be it known to you all… that by the name of our Lord Jesus Christ of Nazareth, whom you crucified, whom God hath raised from the dead, even by him, this man standeth here before you whole… Neither is there salvation in any other. For there is no other name under heaven given to men, whereby we must be saved’ (4:10, 12).",
 who="Since only Jesus’ name saves, asking the saints for help is calling on other names. Catholics pray the Litany of the Saints; that is calling on many names.",
 lang="“Name” (onoma) in Scripture means the person and his power and authority. ‘In the name of Jesus’ means by his authority and power.",
 steps=[
  ["Catholics affirm it fully","‘There is no salvation outside Christ’ (CCC 846 and the whole Catechism). Every grace the saints received and every grace they obtain for us comes through Christ alone."],
  ["The apostles acted in Jesus’ name","Peter’s point is that the healing came by Jesus’ name, through Peter’s ministry. Christ works through his servants. The same is true of the saints’ intercession: they pray through Christ, and God answers through Christ."],
  ["Asking a saint’s prayer is asking for prayer in Jesus’ name","The Church’s liturgical prayer to the saints asks: ‘pray for us’. They do not save; they pray. Every collect for a saint’s feast ends ‘through our Lord Jesus Christ’."],
  ["The context is the Sanhedrin’s rejection of Jesus","Peter tells the religious authorities that they cannot be saved by rejecting Jesus: no other Messiah, no other way of salvation exists. The verse is about Christ’s uniqueness, which Catholics proclaim."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Cyril of Jerusalem, Mystagogical Catecheses 5.9 (c. 350)","We commemorate the saints so that God, at their prayers and intercessions, would receive our petition (paraphrased): their prayer is always directed to God."],
  ["CCC 2666–2668","The name of Jesus is at the heart of Christian prayer; all liturgical prayer concludes ‘through our Lord Jesus Christ’."]],
 obj=[
  ["“A litany calling on dozens of saints obscures Christ.”","Each invocation is ‘pray for us’; the litany begins with ‘Lord, have mercy; Christ, have mercy’ and addresses the Trinity directly. It is the communion of saints praying together through Christ."]],
 talk="“Catholics believe Jesus is the only Saviour, period. The saints aren’t saviours; they were saved by him. When we ask them to pray for us, they pray in Jesus’ name, just like when you ask your friends to pray. Peter healed that lame man through his own ministry, but in Jesus’ name. That’s how God works: through his people, always through Jesus.”",
 avoid="Do not use language that suggests saints save; say they pray for us.",
 read=["CCC 432, 846, 956, 2666–2668","Lumen Gentium 49–50"])

add('revelation',19,10,'Revelation 19:10','"See thou do it not… adore God"',
 'John fell down to worship an angel and was rebuked. So it is wrong to bow before or honour angels and saints.',
 ['John, an apostle, did this twice (19:10; 22:8–9). He would have known not to worship a creature; he was overwhelmed by a being he may have taken for divine, or showed a reverence the angel humbly declined.',
  'The angel\'s reason is that he is a "fellow servant" of John and of those who have "the testimony of Jesus". Worship belongs to God alone.',
  'Elsewhere Scripture shows reverence to angels without rebuke: Joshua falls on his face before the commander of the Lord\'s army (Joshua 5:14–15); Lot bows before angels (Genesis 19:1).',
  'Catholics worship God alone (latria) and honour angels and saints (dulia). This verse condemns worship, not honour.'],
 'Adoration is for God alone; the angels and saints are our fellow servants, whom we honour and with whom we worship.',
 'CCC 2096–2097, 2132; ST II-II q.84, q.103',
 tags=('saints',))
D('revelation',19,10,
 ctx="Revelation 19:9–10: the angel tells John to write ‘Blessed are they that are called to the marriage supper of the Lamb’. ‘And I fell down before his feet, to adore him. And he saith to me: See thou do it not: I am thy fellowservant, and of thy brethren, who have the testimony of Jesus. Adore God. For the testimony of Jesus is the spirit of prophecy.’ The same happens in 22:8–9.",
 who="Used against any reverence (kneeling, bowing, praying) toward angels, saints or Mary: if the apostle John was forbidden to fall before an angel, Catholics are forbidden to kneel before statues of saints or pray to them.",
 lang="“Adore” (proskynēsai): prostrate oneself; here in the sense of worship, since the angel answers ‘Adore God’.",
 steps=[
  ["What the angel forbids is worship","The angel’s reply contrasts himself (‘fellow servant’) with God (‘Adore God’). He forbids treating him as divine. Catholics agree: only God receives latria (CCC 2096)."],
  ["John’s action shows how overwhelming the vision was","John, an apostle who had written ‘Little children, keep yourselves from idols’ (1 John 5:21), would not knowingly commit idolatry. He fell before the angel twice, perhaps mistaking him for the Lord or giving excessive reverence. The angel corrects with humility, as a fellow servant."],
  ["Reverence to angels is shown elsewhere without rebuke","Joshua falls on his face before the commander of the Lord’s army, who tells him to take off his shoes (Joshua 5:14–15). Lot bows to the ground before the two angels (Genesis 19:1). Balaam bows before the angel (Numbers 22:31). The difference is between honour and worship."],
  ["Angels and saints are fellow servants with whom we worship","The angel’s words express the communion of saints: angels, prophets and believers are one family serving God. At every Mass the Church sings the Sanctus ‘with the angels and archangels and with all the company of heaven’."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Augustine, City of God X.7 (c. 420)","The holy angels do not wish us to sacrifice to them but to God, whose sacrifice they know themselves to be along with us (paraphrased)."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, ST II-II q.84 a.1 ad 1; q.103 a.3–4","Adoration due to God is latria; the reverence due to creatures of excellence is dulia, distinct in kind."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Bowing to statues is exactly what John did.”","John’s act was meant as adoration, which the angel refused. A Catholic bowing before a statue of a saint is giving honour, not adoration, and the saint is not even physically present. Intention decides."]],
 talk="“The angel refused worship, and Catholics agree: we worship God alone. But reverence isn’t always worship. Joshua fell on his face before an angel and was told to take his shoes off, not rebuked. Catholics honour angels and saints as fellow servants of God, and we worship God together with them at every Mass.”",
 avoid="Do not deny that excessive or superstitious devotion can happen; correct it with the distinction between latria and dulia.",
 read=["CCC 2096–2097, 2132, 335","ST II-II q.84; q.103","Joshua 5:13–15"])

add('romans',8,1,'Romans 8:1','"There is now no condemnation"',
 'Christians are free from condemnation, so they cannot be punished after death. Purgatory, as a place of punishment, contradicts this.',
 ['Purgatory is not condemnation. Souls in purgatory are saved; condemnation means hell.',
  'The verse in the Douay (and KJV) adds "who walk not according to the flesh": the promise belongs to those who live in the Spirit. Many modern Bibles end the verse earlier, but 8:4 has the same clause.',
  'Romans 8 itself says "if you live according to the flesh, you shall die" (8:13).',
  'Purgatory is the purifying love of God completing the healing of the saved, not a sentence of guilt.'],
 'No condemnation for those in Christ; purification for those not yet perfect.',
 'CCC 1030–1032; 1 Corinthians 3:13–15',
 disp='d-purgatory',tags=('purgatory',))
D('romans',8,1,
 ctx="Romans 7 describes the struggle of a person under the Law, ending in ‘Unhappy man that I am, who shall deliver me from the body of this death? The grace of God, by Jesus Christ our Lord’ (7:24–25). Romans 8:1–4: ‘There is now therefore no condemnation to them that are in Christ Jesus, who walk not according to the flesh. For the law of the spirit of life, in Christ Jesus, hath delivered me from the law of sin and of death… that the justification of the law might be fulfilled in us, who walk not according to the flesh, but according to the spirit.’",
 who="Used against purgatory and against the Catholic distinction between eternal and temporal punishment: if no condemnation remains, no punishment remains either.",
 lang="“Condemnation” (katakrima): a sentence of condemnation, the verdict of guilt leading to penalty, particularly death (5:16, 18).\n\nTextual note: the clause ‘who walk not according to the flesh’ in 8:1 is in the Vulgate and the Byzantine manuscripts (hence Douay and KJV); the earliest manuscripts lack it there, and modern translations omit it. It is certainly original in 8:4.",
 steps=[
  ["Purgatory is for those not under condemnation","The Church teaches that all in purgatory ‘are assured of their eternal salvation’ (CCC 1030). They are in Christ, free from condemnation. Purgatory is not a lesser hell or a sentence of guilt."],
  ["Discipline is not condemnation","Hebrews 12:6–11: ‘whom the Lord loveth, he chastiseth… for the present all chastisement seemeth to bring not joy, but sorrow: but afterwards it will yield… the most peaceable fruit of justice’. God disciplines his children because they are his, not because they are condemned. Purgatory is the final form of this purifying love (Spe Salvi 47)."],
  ["The promise is to those who walk in the Spirit","Romans 8 makes the condition explicit (8:4, 13). It is not a guarantee that someone who lives according to the flesh escapes condemnation."],
  ["1 Corinthians 3 describes a saved person suffering loss","‘If any man’s work burn, he shall suffer loss; but he himself shall be saved, yet so as by fire’ (1 Corinthians 3:15). Saved, not condemned, yet suffering loss and passing through fire."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Augustine, Exposition on Psalm 37.3 (c. 400)","Some will be saved through fire; and though saved, that fire will be more grievous than anything a man can suffer in this life (paraphrased)."],
  ["Benedict XVI, Spe Salvi 47 (2007)","The encounter with Christ is the decisive act of judgment; his gaze burns and transforms us, a blessed pain in which his love penetrates us like a flame (paraphrased)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“If Christ took all punishment, none remains.”","Christ took the eternal punishment of sin. Scripture shows forgiven people still undergoing temporal consequences and discipline (2 Samuel 12:13–14; Hebrews 12). Purgatory belongs to that category: healing, not condemnation."]],
 talk="“Purgatory isn’t condemnation at all. Everyone in purgatory is saved and on their way to heaven. It’s God finishing the job of making us holy, like the discipline Hebrews 12 talks about: painful, but because he loves us as his children. Paul describes it in 1 Corinthians 3: someone saved, ‘yet as through fire’.”",
 avoid="Do not describe purgatory in terms that sound like punishment by an angry judge; stress love and purification.",
 read=["CCC 1030–1032","Benedict XVI (2007) Spe Salvi 45–48","Hebrews 12:5–11; 1 Corinthians 3:10–15"])

add('hebrews',9,27,'Hebrews 9:27','"Once to die, and after this the judgment"',
 'Death is followed immediately by judgment, which settles each person\'s destiny for good. There is no room for an intermediate purgatory or for prayers to help the dead.',
 ['Catholic teaching agrees: at death each person receives the particular judgment, and his eternal destiny is fixed (CCC 1021–1022).',
  'Purgatory comes after the judgment for those judged to be saved but not yet perfect. It does not change anyone\'s destiny; it prepares the saved for heaven.',
  'Hebrews 9:27 does not describe what follows judgment for the saved; Hebrews 12:23 speaks of "the spirits of the just made perfect", implying perfecting.',
  'The verse also rules out reincarnation, which Catholics also reject.'],
 'Judgment at death decides each soul\'s eternal destiny; purification afterwards prepares the saved for glory.',
 'CCC 1021–1022, 1030–1032',
 disp='d-purgatory',tags=('purgatory',))
D('hebrews',9,27,
 ctx="Hebrews 9:24–28: Christ entered heaven ‘to appear now in the presence of God for us’, not to offer himself often, ‘but now once at the end of ages, he hath appeared for the destruction of sin, by the sacrifice of himself. And as it is appointed unto men once to die, and after this the judgment: so also Christ was offered once to exhaust the sins of many; the second time he shall appear without sin to them that expect him unto salvation’ (9:26–28).",
 who="The sequence ‘death, then judgment’ leaves no room for a time of purification or for prayers after death to make any difference: one’s fate is sealed at death.",
 lang="“Appointed” (apokeitai): laid up, reserved. “Once” (hapax): death happens once. “Judgment” (krisis).",
 steps=[
  ["The verse’s point is the analogy with Christ","As human beings die once, Christ was offered once. The verse is about the uniqueness of Christ’s sacrifice, not a detailed eschatology."],
  ["Catholics affirm judgment immediately after death","The ‘particular judgment’: ‘each man receives his eternal retribution in his immortal soul at the very moment of his death’ (CCC 1022). Heaven (immediately or through purification) or hell."],
  ["Purgatory follows judgment, it does not replace it","The souls in purgatory have been judged and are saved. Purgatory is not a second chance; it is the completion of the work in those already judged to be God’s."],
  ["Hebrews implies perfection after death","Hebrews 12:23: ‘the spirits of the just made perfect (teteleiōmenōn)’. The just are ‘made perfect’, which fits a final purification."],
  ["Prayers for the dead help those already judged as saved","They do not change anyone’s eternal destiny; they help the saved through their purification (2 Maccabees 12:46: ‘It is therefore a holy and wholesome thought to pray for the dead, that they may be loosed from sins’)."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Council of Lyons II (1274) and Benedict XII, Benedictus Deus (1336)","Souls are judged immediately after death; those needing purification are purified, others enter heaven at once, the damned go to hell (paraphrased)."],
  ["CCC 1021–1022","Death puts an end to human life as the time open to accepting or rejecting grace; each person receives particular judgment."]],
 obj=[
  ["“If destiny is fixed, prayer for the dead is pointless.”","Prayer does not change their destiny; it helps their purification, as Christians help one another in this life. The communion of saints continues after death."]],
 talk="“Catholics completely agree: you die once, then you’re judged, and your eternal destiny is settled. Purgatory isn’t a second chance; it’s for people already judged as saved who aren’t perfect yet. Hebrews 12 even talks about ‘the spirits of the just made perfect’. Our prayers help them along the way to heaven.”",
 avoid="Do not imply purgatory is a place where people can still be lost or saved.",
 read=["CCC 1021–1032","Benedict XII (1336) Benedictus Deus","2 Maccabees 12:38–46"])

add('1-timothy',3,2,'1 Timothy 3:2','"A bishop… the husband of one wife"',
 'Paul requires a bishop to be married. So the Catholic requirement of celibacy for bishops and (Latin) priests contradicts Scripture.',
 ['The point is "of one wife": a bishop must not have remarried or be unfaithful. Paul is setting a maximum, not requiring marriage.',
  'Paul himself was unmarried (1 Corinthians 7:7–8) and recommends celibacy for those who can accept it, because the unmarried man "is solicitous for the things that belong to the Lord" (7:32).',
  'Timothy, to whom the letter is written and who ordained presbyters, was apparently unmarried; if the rule required marriage, Paul and Timothy would be disqualified.',
  'Eastern Catholic Churches ordain married men as priests; celibacy in the Latin Church is a discipline, not a doctrine that marriage is wrong.'],
 'Paul forbids remarried or unfaithful men as bishops; he does not command bishops to marry, and he praises celibacy for the kingdom.',
 'CCC 1579–1580; 1 Corinthians 7:7–8, 32–35; Matthew 19:12',
 tags=('priesthood',))
D('1-timothy',3,2,
 ctx="1 Timothy 3:1–7 lists qualifications for a bishop (episkopos): ‘It behoveth therefore a bishop to be blameless, the husband of one wife, sober, prudent, of good behaviour, chaste, given to hospitality, a teacher, not given to wine, no striker, but modest, not quarrelsome, not covetous, but one that ruleth well his own house, having his children in subjection with all chastity’ (3:2–4). Similar lists apply to deacons (3:8–13) and presbyters (Titus 1:5–9).",
 who="Paul’s list requires bishops (and presbyters, Titus 1:6) to be married men with children. Rome’s requirement of celibacy imposes a rule Scripture contradicts, and some add 1 Timothy 4:3 (‘forbidding to marry’).",
 lang="“Husband of one wife” (mias gynaikos andra): literally ‘a one-woman man’. The emphasis falls on ‘one’ (mias). Paul uses the parallel phrase for widows enrolled by the Church: ‘the wife of one husband’ (5:9), meaning married only once.",
 steps=[
  ["The emphasis is ‘one’","‘A one-woman man’ excludes polygamy, divorce and remarriage, and unfaithfulness. The early Church read it as excluding men married twice (digamy), the same sense as ‘wife of one husband’ for widows (5:9). It sets a limit, not a requirement."],
  ["Taken literally, it would disqualify Paul and Timothy","Paul was unmarried and wished others were like him (1 Corinthians 7:7–8). Timothy appears to have been unmarried. If marriage were required, the apostle writing the rule and the bishop receiving it would be disqualified, as would widowers and childless men."],
  ["Paul and Jesus praise celibacy for the kingdom","Jesus: ‘there are eunuchs, who have made themselves eunuchs for the kingdom of heaven. He that can take, let him take it’ (Matthew 19:12). Paul: ‘he that is without a wife, is solicitous for the things that belong to the Lord… but he that is with a wife, is solicitous for the things of the world… and he is divided’ (1 Corinthians 7:32–33). Peter: ‘we have left all things’ (Matthew 19:27–29)."],
  ["Celibacy is a discipline, not doctrine","The Catholic Church has married priests in the Eastern Catholic Churches and some convert clergy in the Latin Church. Celibacy for Latin priests grew from early discipline (the Council of Elvira, c. 305, required clerical continence) and became universal law in the West in the Middle Ages. It is changeable in principle; the Church keeps it because it configures the priest to Christ and frees him for service (CCC 1579–1580)."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Tertullian, To His Wife I.7 (c. 205)","Notes that men married twice are not admitted to the priesthood (paraphrased), the early understanding of ‘husband of one wife’."],
  ["Council of Elvira, canon 33 (c. 305)","Requires bishops, presbyters and deacons to abstain from relations with their wives."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on 1 Timothy, ch. 3, lect. 1","Paul does not command a bishop to have a wife, but forbids one who has had more than one (paraphrased)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Peter was married (Mark 1:30).”","Yes, and Catholics have never said marriage is incompatible with ordination as such; the Eastern Churches show it. But Peter also says he ‘left all things’ to follow Christ (Matthew 19:27), and the discipline of celibacy is a legitimate development the Church may choose."]],
 talk="“The emphasis is on ‘one’: a one-woman man, not remarried or unfaithful. Paul used the same phrase for widows: ‘wife of one husband’. If he meant bishops had to be married, he’d have disqualified himself, since he wasn’t married and wished others were like him. And the Catholic Church does have married priests in the Eastern Catholic Churches; celibacy in the West is a discipline, not a teaching that marriage is bad.”",
 avoid="Do not argue that celibacy is required by divine law; it is not.",
 read=["CCC 1579–1580","1 Corinthians 7","Paul VI (1967) Sacerdotalis Caelibatus","Christian Cochini (1990) Apostolic Origins of Priestly Celibacy. San Francisco: Ignatius Press"])
